**BFS** uses a queue to ensure nodes are processed in order of distance from the source. The algorithm:

1. Enqueue the source node, mark it visited.
2. While the queue is not empty: dequeue the front node, process it, enqueue all unvisited neighbors and mark them visited immediately.

The 'mark visited before enqueue, not after dequeue' rule is critical: if you mark after dequeue, the same node can be enqueued multiple times (once from each neighbor that discovers it), causing O(V²) work instead of O(V + E).

```javascript
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const queue = [start];
  let head = 0; // pointer-based dequeue: no shift needed
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor); // mark before enqueue
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Level Tracking

To track which level (distance) each node is at, record the queue's size at the start of each level. Process exactly that many nodes for the current level, then everything newly enqueued is the next level.

A **deque** (double-ended queue) supports O(1) push and pop at both the front and the back. JavaScript doesn't have a built-in deque, but we can simulate one with an array and a `front` index pointer for the left end (amortized O(1) per left-pop).

## Monotonic Deque for Sliding Window Maximum

For a window of size `k` sliding across an array, the naive approach to find the maximum each step is O(k) per slide → O(nk) total. The **monotonic deque** gives O(n) total.

Maintain a deque of **indices** such that the corresponding values are in **decreasing order** from front to back. For each new element at index `right`:
1. Remove from the front if the front index is outside the current window (`front <= right - k`).
2. Remove from the back any index whose value is smaller than the current value - the current element dominates them for all future windows.
3. Push `right` to the back.
4. The front of the deque is the index of the maximum in the current window.

```javascript
function slidingWindowMax(arr, k) {
  const deque = []; // indices, values decrease front to back
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (deque.length && deque[0] <= i - k) deque.shift(); // expired
    while (deque.length && arr[deque[deque.length-1]] < arr[i]) deque.pop(); // dominated
    deque.push(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) result.push(arr[deque[0]]);
  }
  return result;
}
```

**Multi-source BFS** starts BFS from multiple source nodes simultaneously. Instead of enqueuing a single start node, enqueue ALL source nodes at once before beginning the main loop. Each source is treated as if it were at distance 0.

## Why It Works

BFS always expands in order of distance. When all sources are enqueued at distance 0, the BFS naturally propagates outward from all of them simultaneously. Each cell is reached first by its nearest source - because BFS guarantees no shorter path will be found later.

## Example: Distance to Nearest 0 in a Binary Grid

Enqueue all positions where `grid[i][j] === 0` with distance 0. Run BFS: each 1-cell is reached by the nearest 0-cell first.

```javascript
const queue = [];
for (let i = 0; i < m; i++)
  for (let j = 0; j < n; j++)
    if (grid[i][j] === 0) { queue.push([i, j]); dist[i][j] = 0; }
// then standard BFS from all sources in queue
```

